1
00:00:02,728 --> 00:00:14,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, his restraint was like denial of the entire domain of the senses, not just restraint in regard to the unwholesome, in regard to sensual.

2
00:00:15,433 --> 00:00:20,935
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it was like the simile of the bait, that sutta, where you kind of completely shun the bait, you shun everything.

3
00:00:21,736 --> 00:00:27,578
[SPEAKER_01]: You see the danger, but you can't, like, stay within it, you know, in a careful manner.

4
00:00:27,818 --> 00:00:29,779
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you cut deep, you throw the whole thing away.

5
00:00:30,854 --> 00:00:33,455
[SPEAKER_01]: In sooner or later, you have to come back to it.

6
00:00:33,475 --> 00:00:42,757
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like when people, like, you just strain, sort of restrain, and then just, poof, give in, and then, you know, the dam breaks, and then just create worse mess than before.

7
00:00:44,097 --> 00:00:46,298
[SPEAKER_01]: Uh, so it's kind of oscillating between those two ends.

8
00:00:47,778 --> 00:00:56,980
[SPEAKER_01]: And I, in that talk, when I said, you know, like a, like, say, like your senses, say, a visual sense, it's like a wild dog, that wants to be courier.

9
00:00:58,425 --> 00:01:03,768
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sometimes, okay, by default, you resist the pull.

10
00:01:03,868 --> 00:01:08,971
[SPEAKER_01]: But sometimes, you can't constantly be resisting the pull, because it will wear you down.

11
00:01:09,852 --> 00:01:11,333
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you have to pick your battles.

12
00:01:11,813 --> 00:01:15,115
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever you resist the pull, there is clearly unwholesome.

13
00:01:16,176 --> 00:01:17,877
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no negotiation there.

14
00:01:18,437 --> 00:01:21,659
[SPEAKER_01]: However, if that wild dog is pulling you towards something that is neutral,

15
00:01:22,630 --> 00:01:25,211
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to be careful to let, like, not resist that.

16
00:01:25,791 --> 00:01:27,632
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's easy to give in and say, no.

17
00:01:28,012 --> 00:01:29,612
[SPEAKER_01]: I say no to everything.

18
00:01:30,032 --> 00:01:34,034
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like what monks usually do when they ordain or something and they come and that's when they become neurotic.

19
00:01:34,834 --> 00:01:36,814
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, vinaya, vinaya.

20
00:01:36,834 --> 00:01:37,055
[SPEAKER_01]: What?

21
00:01:37,075 --> 00:01:38,635
[SPEAKER_01]: No, vinaya actually says the opposite.

22
00:01:39,595 --> 00:01:40,376
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, but still.

23
00:01:41,016 --> 00:01:42,116
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no, but still.

24
00:01:42,136 --> 00:01:44,877
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to allow for that space in between.

25
00:01:46,465 --> 00:01:53,070
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the whole thing with the way that we spoke before, with like, putting the cloth down in women, it's actually not properly seen before.

26
00:01:54,651 --> 00:01:58,134
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can exactly see how it originated, was from

27
00:01:59,161 --> 00:02:02,843
[SPEAKER_01]: A woman might have accidentally brushed against your fingers when you were receiving.

28
00:02:03,043 --> 00:02:04,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing to do with you.

29
00:02:04,424 --> 00:02:08,467
[SPEAKER_01]: And the vinaya says clearly, if that thing happens, it's not an offense.

30
00:02:08,867 --> 00:02:09,747
[SPEAKER_01]: You did not intend it.

31
00:02:10,008 --> 00:02:18,613
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, obviously, you know, a doubtful mind, well, a mind that is not developed in regard to doubt, will be overwhelmed by that doubt eventually.

32
00:02:19,313 --> 00:02:21,935
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you know that really you haven't done anything wrong.

33
00:02:22,215 --> 00:02:24,576
[SPEAKER_01]: The Vinaya says that you haven't done anything wrong.

34
00:02:24,616 --> 00:02:25,717
[SPEAKER_01]: It was unintentional.

35
00:02:26,077 --> 00:02:33,842
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't sleep at night, because basically you're caught in this neurotic, kind of doubting, paranoid, sort of... whatever, spiraling out of control.

36
00:02:34,382 --> 00:02:35,163
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, what you do?

37
00:02:35,403 --> 00:02:42,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you, like, make an extra step and you create your own rule and... to prevent even that from happening.

38
00:02:43,289 --> 00:02:43,689
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no.

39
00:02:44,510 --> 00:02:45,350
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll put a cloth down.

40
00:02:45,630 --> 00:02:48,211
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll put a cloth, so then the woman can't touch me accidentally.

41
00:02:48,532 --> 00:02:50,272
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but being touched accidentally is not a problem.

42
00:02:50,653 --> 00:03:00,057
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if that is bothering you, you should deal with that on that phenomenological level, not like on the level of running away and imposing another rule on top of a rule.

43
00:03:00,277 --> 00:03:01,318
[SPEAKER_01]: And that rule is unnecessary.

44
00:03:02,258 --> 00:03:02,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Most certainly.

45
00:03:03,219 --> 00:03:04,579
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it can be relevant.

46
00:03:04,880 --> 00:03:05,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

47
00:03:05,320 --> 00:03:06,780
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you use a cloth or whatever.

48
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:09,822
[SPEAKER_01]: But, like, in this case, for example, it actually goes against the vinaya rule.

49
00:03:11,332 --> 00:03:16,119
[SPEAKER_01]: So, because of your neurosis, vinaya became inadequate for you, and you need more.

50
00:03:16,279 --> 00:03:17,801
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is exactly what happens in the monastery.

51
00:03:17,821 --> 00:03:18,422
[SPEAKER_01]: The core of art.

52
00:03:20,886 --> 00:03:25,252
[SPEAKER_01]: In the sense of seeing a wild dog that's pulling me out, that's not a problem.

53
00:03:26,061 --> 00:03:29,724
[SPEAKER_01]: Problem is, when it ends up in unwholesome states, and then I have to suffer and counter it.

54
00:03:30,305 --> 00:03:31,526
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still the same wild dog.

55
00:03:31,566 --> 00:03:32,106
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not mine.

56
00:03:32,206 --> 00:03:32,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Never was mine.

57
00:03:32,887 --> 00:03:33,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Never can be mine.

58
00:03:34,488 --> 00:03:42,935
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, you just basically foresee the unwholesome scenarios where that dog will go, and you prevent that, and then let it go, and then the dog will eventually calm down as well.

59
00:03:44,737 --> 00:03:50,321
[SPEAKER_01]: But you mustn't go around just saying no to everything you see, everything you hear, everything that you might doubt, because you might doubt everything.

60
00:03:50,481 --> 00:03:54,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's just going to be a very tight and unpleasant state, and it is.

61
00:03:55,425 --> 00:03:55,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Always.

62
00:03:55,806 --> 00:03:56,486
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no exception.

63
00:03:57,307 --> 00:03:58,769
[SPEAKER_01]: So, people do suffer, on the contrary.

64
00:03:59,149 --> 00:04:05,376
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, even when you say, you have to avoid unwholesome, don't necessarily worry about wholesome.

65
00:04:06,840 --> 00:04:12,482
[SPEAKER_01]: It's quite hard for them to allow that, because that literally means taking up responsibility for the distinction.

66
00:04:13,163 --> 00:04:20,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so, now I have to rely on my own criteria, that this is unwholesome and this is wholesome.

67
00:04:20,446 --> 00:04:25,968
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no external authority, there is no external community, external monastery, external vinaya, external this.

68
00:04:26,508 --> 00:04:35,192
[SPEAKER_01]: That's kind of beforehand, but now, I actually need to draw a line between good and between the bad, and then stay behind that line.

69
00:04:36,412 --> 00:04:38,794
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's basically a very hard thing to do.

70
00:04:39,114 --> 00:04:43,196
[SPEAKER_01]: But if a person does that, then they truly go beyond sīla-vāt-paramāsa.

71
00:04:43,316 --> 00:04:48,980
[SPEAKER_01]: And sīla-vāt-paramāsa, as I'm sure I've said before, it's not what people commonly think of rites and rituals.

72
00:04:49,140 --> 00:04:52,202
[SPEAKER_01]: Some ridiculous bowing to a gold-gilded shrine or something.

73
00:04:52,422 --> 00:04:52,602
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

74
00:04:52,802 --> 00:04:54,884
[SPEAKER_01]: It's holding to virtue and duty.

75
00:04:55,804 --> 00:04:57,284
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is quite a serious thing.

76
00:04:57,464 --> 00:04:59,325
[SPEAKER_01]: As things that will actually free you.

77
00:04:59,425 --> 00:04:59,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

78
00:04:59,885 --> 00:05:05,767
[SPEAKER_01]: As things that are directly resulting in liberation, which they don't do.

79
00:05:06,027 --> 00:05:09,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, you believe that your virtue and duty will directly lead you to nirvana.

80
00:05:09,748 --> 00:05:10,088
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't.

81
00:05:10,488 --> 00:05:10,888
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't.

82
00:05:11,268 --> 00:05:11,648
[SPEAKER_01]: It couldn't.

83
00:05:12,548 --> 00:05:14,109
[SPEAKER_01]: It can enable you to draw a line.

84
00:05:14,729 --> 00:05:15,769
[SPEAKER_01]: The line I was talking about.

85
00:05:16,249 --> 00:05:22,611
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you draw that line, then virtue and duty, which you have adopted beforehand, and you have to be there, you have to adopt it beforehand,

86
00:05:23,480 --> 00:05:29,305
[SPEAKER_01]: But once you draw a line, once you got the criteria for that line, it falls away, as virtue, as duty.

87
00:05:30,366 --> 00:05:34,629
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it is beyond the sīla, sotāpanna and then eventually arānta.

88
00:05:36,791 --> 00:05:43,756
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why it's hard for people to draw that line, because it relies on your own judgment.

89
00:05:45,097 --> 00:05:52,023
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you know, there is no safety of tradition, safety of the history, the order of everything.

90
00:05:52,567 --> 00:05:55,348
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just you, basically, sticking behind what you chose to do.

91
00:05:55,368 --> 00:06:10,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, you won't be able to wholesomely, or rather rightly, draw that line, if you haven't been adopting, even externally, these rites and rituals, or rather virtue and duty, the norms.

92
00:06:11,875 --> 00:06:23,324
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you must certainly not stay there, because then, yeah, then you will be like, you know, a fifty-fifty Vāsa monk who is still as neurotic about his rule as on the first day, and still not in the sotā.

93
00:06:24,825 --> 00:06:29,248
[SPEAKER_00]: But there's a... going beyond sīla means going deeper.

94
00:06:29,628 --> 00:06:34,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, going beyond sīla means basically not depending on it, but not breaking it either.

95
00:06:34,852 --> 00:06:35,032
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

96
00:06:35,052 --> 00:06:37,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, being unable to break it, to an extent, obviously.

97
00:06:38,183 --> 00:06:40,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, that sīla is also defined in regard to...

98
00:06:40,904 --> 00:06:58,793
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereas in sīla, I mean, like, all the necessary little... all the little circumstantial vinaya rules, I talk about the basic principles of, you know, celibacy, lying, stealing, things that are, in themselves, unwholesome.

99
00:06:58,893 --> 00:07:06,997
[SPEAKER_01]: So that is the importance of it, because obviously, you know, virtue is important, but then people take it, and then

100
00:07:07,943 --> 00:07:15,709
[SPEAKER_01]: Even after, you know, all the knowledge you might have gained through suttas or whatever, they're still unable to let go of it, because they don't know they should.

101
00:07:16,070 --> 00:07:19,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, they fear if they let go, it means they go the opposite of non-sīla.

102
00:07:19,813 --> 00:07:23,956
[SPEAKER_01]: And that can happen if that sīla hasn't been established beforehand.

103
00:07:24,837 --> 00:07:25,497
[SPEAKER_01]: But if it was...

104
00:07:25,517 --> 00:07:37,287
[SPEAKER_01]: So there is that... You have to... Basically, it's like building up a ship, developing sails, you know, gathering all the information about the design and engineering it, and then doing the whole thing.

105
00:07:37,710 --> 00:07:40,932
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, never putting it into water and trying it out.

106
00:07:42,493 --> 00:07:42,813
[SPEAKER_01]: Never.

107
00:07:43,673 --> 00:07:47,375
[SPEAKER_01]: Never, like, setting it to go, to see if it's gonna sink.

108
00:07:47,395 --> 00:07:55,319
[SPEAKER_01]: So, start sinking, quickly pull it back, repair it, but you have to cross the other shore, not remain on this, clutching onto your little boat.

109
00:07:56,920 --> 00:08:02,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Because what's the use of the boat if you don't actually use it to sail away into the sea?

110
00:08:05,126 --> 00:08:15,151
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in terms of the food, that's like the... that's quite a point that you've seen that yourself, probably experienced that yourself in the monasteries, how people like...

111
00:08:15,931 --> 00:08:19,092
[SPEAKER_01]: So, okay, I have to eat.

112
00:08:19,272 --> 00:08:25,035
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like, you know, sexual implications or other unwholesome things that you don't have to do and you shouldn't.

113
00:08:25,155 --> 00:08:26,176
[SPEAKER_01]: But the food I have to eat.

114
00:08:27,096 --> 00:08:29,237
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, obviously, you always have preferences.

115
00:08:31,063 --> 00:08:38,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the mouth, your tongue, the sense of taste as an independent animal is an independent animal.

116
00:08:39,028 --> 00:08:40,769
[SPEAKER_01]: It prefers things, it wants things.

117
00:08:41,009 --> 00:08:42,249
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't want certain other things.

118
00:08:43,610 --> 00:08:50,074
[SPEAKER_01]: And that has kind of evolved completely circumstantially.

119
00:08:51,428 --> 00:08:58,295
[SPEAKER_01]: Based on the stuff you were eating, you develop taste for certain things, you got sick of some other taste, that's it.

120
00:08:58,795 --> 00:09:02,279
[SPEAKER_01]: But obviously, for kutujana, it's inseparable from the sense of self.

121
00:09:03,780 --> 00:09:05,782
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, when somebody says, OK, you need to eat carefully,

122
00:09:06,497 --> 00:09:12,780
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha said you have to eat, but you have to eat carefully, like it's your last... you know, you killed your child, you need to eat just for survival.

123
00:09:13,120 --> 00:09:14,300
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, that is true.

124
00:09:14,340 --> 00:09:18,142
[SPEAKER_01]: If you want to, like, practice for arhantship, that is absolutely the attitude you have to have.

125
00:09:18,462 --> 00:09:22,384
[SPEAKER_01]: I eat food purely, so this body will still be alive, so that I can practice.

126
00:09:24,204 --> 00:09:29,807
[SPEAKER_01]: But nowhere there does it say you need to be obsessed about your preferences, or certainly try to avoid them.

127
00:09:30,327 --> 00:09:33,108
[SPEAKER_01]: So you have people who get so wound up, neurotic, about

128
00:09:34,975 --> 00:09:36,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Eating food that they like.

129
00:09:37,136 --> 00:09:42,317
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I can't be mindful, basically, if the food I'm eating is the food I like.

130
00:09:42,597 --> 00:09:43,217
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible.

131
00:09:43,437 --> 00:09:44,238
[SPEAKER_01]: But actually it isn't.

132
00:09:45,178 --> 00:09:50,239
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness has nothing to do with whether what you're eating is what you prefer or what you don't prefer.

133
00:09:50,799 --> 00:09:53,460
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness is actually indifferent to your preference.

134
00:09:53,940 --> 00:09:55,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why it's the mindfulness that makes you correct.

135
00:09:56,001 --> 00:09:57,661
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not just the opposite preference.

136
00:09:58,662 --> 00:10:03,443
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I remember that I met people who just intentionally take

137
00:10:04,428 --> 00:10:06,469
[SPEAKER_01]: Opposite of what they really want to take.

138
00:10:07,889 --> 00:10:11,590
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like, well, the whole purpose of that is only to avoid responsibility for what you've taken.

139
00:10:12,750 --> 00:10:13,971
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no mindfulness involved.

140
00:10:15,231 --> 00:10:18,912
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just you feel responsible if you take what you want to take.

141
00:10:18,972 --> 00:10:23,313
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you think you avoid that responsibility by then taking what you don't want to take.

142
00:10:23,393 --> 00:10:26,794
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't see that you're responsible for that now.

143
00:10:26,834 --> 00:10:30,215
[SPEAKER_01]: It means you can't avoid your responsibility by choosing a particular choice.

144
00:10:31,750 --> 00:10:34,631
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize, why did I choose what I prefer or what I don't?

145
00:10:34,991 --> 00:10:37,592
[SPEAKER_01]: I am affected by the choice of choosing the food.

146
00:10:38,712 --> 00:10:46,415
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if I write down an impersonal algorithm that calculates for me what number of dishes to take on one day, I have...

147
00:10:46,435 --> 00:10:51,737
[SPEAKER_01]: I have basically chosen the algorithm to be that which can give me the food to eat.

148
00:10:52,417 --> 00:10:54,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, I'm still responsible for it.

149
00:10:55,133 --> 00:11:11,061
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, when you rely on an external thing, an external, like an algorithm, or external written law, or somebody what's said, externally said, you will not be aware internally of your responsibility for your choices, which means you won't be mindful properly.

150
00:11:12,001 --> 00:11:15,723
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you realize, I think, you don't actually have to fear your preferences.

151
00:11:16,443 --> 00:11:19,665
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to fear losing mindfulness in regard to that.

152
00:11:20,447 --> 00:11:23,469
[SPEAKER_01]: Or in regard to animals, that's when the issues set in.

153
00:11:24,049 --> 00:11:26,230
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when a video gets rekindled and solved.

154
00:11:26,790 --> 00:11:28,491
[SPEAKER_01]: Not when you eat your favorite fruit.

155
00:11:30,952 --> 00:11:35,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Because people... people might say, I want to give a bit of my attachment towards food.

156
00:11:35,535 --> 00:11:35,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

157
00:11:37,156 --> 00:11:39,697
[SPEAKER_01]: But they identify their attachment with their preference.

158
00:11:39,897 --> 00:11:40,377
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not.

159
00:11:40,397 --> 00:11:41,998
[SPEAKER_01]: Preference is a preference of your taste.

160
00:11:42,478 --> 00:11:43,859
[SPEAKER_01]: And your taste is not yours, actually.

161
00:11:44,519 --> 00:11:45,920
[SPEAKER_01]: That animal is independent of it.

162
00:11:46,583 --> 00:11:51,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, dragging around for so long, then now you're just... well, you're inseparable from it, in your mind.

163
00:11:52,004 --> 00:11:54,745
[SPEAKER_01]: But even then, your preference is not your attachment.

164
00:11:55,145 --> 00:11:55,825
[SPEAKER_01]: It's ignorance.

165
00:11:55,945 --> 00:11:58,926
[SPEAKER_01]: In regard to preferences, then you're attached, right there.

166
00:11:59,406 --> 00:12:10,168
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the thing, because people, in the course of their life, got so used to... eating food that you prefer means eating it unmindfully, unwarily, lustfully.

167
00:12:12,648 --> 00:12:14,709
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, now, preference means that.

168
00:12:15,925 --> 00:12:17,906
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I must avoid presence.

169
00:12:18,667 --> 00:12:19,227
[SPEAKER_01]: I must deny.

170
00:12:19,407 --> 00:12:20,989
[SPEAKER_01]: I must stop eating altogether.

171
00:12:21,069 --> 00:12:22,530
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like what happened to giants.

172
00:12:23,150 --> 00:12:32,757
[SPEAKER_01]: You could see the revolt, the rebellion of the mind against the preference or the choice, but it went completely the opposite way, within the same domain of choice.

173
00:12:33,137 --> 00:12:35,379
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, they just started choosing the opposite, more and more bizarre.

174
00:12:35,399 --> 00:12:37,720
[SPEAKER_01]: So, oh, I will eat every second day.

175
00:12:37,780 --> 00:12:37,980
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

176
00:12:38,020 --> 00:12:39,101
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, I will eat every third day.

177
00:12:39,181 --> 00:12:39,762
[SPEAKER_01]: When that becomes

178
00:12:40,362 --> 00:12:40,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Not enough.

179
00:12:41,322 --> 00:12:46,584
[SPEAKER_01]: I will eat every third day, from every fourth person, on every third junction of the second town.

180
00:12:46,844 --> 00:12:48,425
[SPEAKER_01]: This is completely randomly evolved.

181
00:12:48,445 --> 00:12:49,385
[SPEAKER_01]: Heavenly rice.

182
00:12:49,405 --> 00:12:49,985
[SPEAKER_01]: And only rice.

183
00:12:50,766 --> 00:12:57,568
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when that basically seems to induce novelty of the restraint... And you find you still lustful for it.

184
00:12:57,628 --> 00:12:58,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

185
00:12:58,088 --> 00:12:58,708
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you do then?

186
00:12:58,728 --> 00:12:59,429
[SPEAKER_01]: You just go further.

187
00:12:59,509 --> 00:13:01,429
[SPEAKER_01]: And then I will stop eating altogether and I will die.

188
00:13:01,489 --> 00:13:02,490
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the highest nibbāna.

189
00:13:02,570 --> 00:13:02,950
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not.

190
00:13:03,965 --> 00:13:13,671
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, as that certain Buddha said, we were studying in a different context, well, in relation to a different question, when he asked me, why do you think that my disciples respect me?

191
00:13:13,691 --> 00:13:17,293
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, well, because you eat little and you praise it in little, you live under a tree, you praise it.

192
00:13:17,313 --> 00:13:18,194
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, no, that's not true.

193
00:13:18,914 --> 00:13:20,195
[SPEAKER_01]: I sometimes eat a bowl.

194
00:13:21,380 --> 00:13:25,405
[SPEAKER_01]: full of food up to the brim of the like softest, nicest rice and curries.

195
00:13:25,666 --> 00:13:28,249
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, I have another bowl full of food.

196
00:13:28,349 --> 00:13:29,831
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he has two alms bowls.

197
00:13:30,392 --> 00:13:32,635
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he finishes a full alms bowl of food.

198
00:13:32,675 --> 00:13:34,718
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, they ask him, oh, Lord, would you like some?

199
00:13:34,918 --> 00:13:35,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, sure.

200
00:13:36,260 --> 00:13:36,921
[SPEAKER_01]: Fill it up again.

201
00:13:37,381 --> 00:13:37,862
[SPEAKER_01]: And he eats it.

202
00:13:38,507 --> 00:13:49,171
[SPEAKER_01]: And while, at the same time, he has disciples who live... who get food every second day or something, and it's a very coarse and horrible food, and they live covered in like a hemp robes and under a root of tree.

203
00:13:49,451 --> 00:13:54,053
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if they were to respect him on account of renouncing behavior, they wouldn't.

204
00:13:55,032 --> 00:13:56,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, they have the utmost respect for him.

205
00:13:56,673 --> 00:13:56,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

206
00:13:56,933 --> 00:13:59,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the dhamma is... well, it's beyond that as well.

207
00:14:00,154 --> 00:14:00,754
[SPEAKER_01]: And they know it.

208
00:14:01,214 --> 00:14:02,575
[SPEAKER_01]: And they know it because of his teaching.

209
00:14:02,595 --> 00:14:04,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, the respect is unshakable.

210
00:14:04,976 --> 00:14:09,377
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if the Buddha just spent the rest of his days living in king's palaces and eating all the time.

211
00:14:09,777 --> 00:14:10,698
[SPEAKER_01]: Makes no difference.

212
00:14:11,558 --> 00:14:21,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you're trying to adjust your food, to adjust your mind, you're taking it from the wrong end.

213
00:14:22,195 --> 00:14:23,315
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you're taking it far enough.

214
00:14:23,335 --> 00:14:26,896
[SPEAKER_01]: You're trying to solve the problem from a community point of view.

215
00:14:26,956 --> 00:14:28,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Or mix, you know, just mix your food in the bowl.

216
00:14:29,477 --> 00:14:41,100
[SPEAKER_01]: I told you, I was doing that because people were talking about it, and I thought, okay, let's... And I didn't mind, to begin with, but I did find it quite amusing, that on the second day of mixing everything, fish,

217
00:14:41,550 --> 00:14:43,252
[SPEAKER_01]: fruit yogurt, grapes and stuff.

218
00:14:43,812 --> 00:14:46,575
[SPEAKER_01]: I started developing tastes for certain mixes.

219
00:14:47,295 --> 00:14:47,676
[SPEAKER_01]: I started...

220
00:14:47,696 --> 00:14:57,745
[SPEAKER_01]: I like fish with grapes and... all I... with my mouth is full of all sorts of like... like fruit, curry, rice and... and... and ice cream, whatever was melted over.

221
00:14:58,585 --> 00:14:59,086
[SPEAKER_01]: My mind

222
00:14:59,717 --> 00:15:07,918
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, if you eat unmindfully, if you lapse, your mind gets focused on the bit of the mouth that is in contact with the taste that you like.

223
00:15:08,398 --> 00:15:28,162
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is no end to that separation of your prefer... well, not preference, of your... like, choice, basically, of what you want, what you like, because you can't do that mechanically, because the mind, the sense, will always pick on that, even if you give it... and then, if you just eat what you don't want, within that, you'll start preferring things, like, what you dislike less.

224
00:15:29,940 --> 00:15:31,661
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's completely from the wrong end.

225
00:15:32,441 --> 00:15:35,643
[SPEAKER_01]: What you need to do is eat mindfully whatever comes your way.

226
00:15:36,483 --> 00:15:40,845
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, if you do need a criteria for, okay, but still, what should I eat?

227
00:15:40,945 --> 00:15:47,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, I'm in a shop as a layman, perhaps, who keeps the precepts, but I'm in a supermarket, I don't...

228
00:15:47,788 --> 00:15:48,888
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what criteria?

229
00:15:48,908 --> 00:15:49,769
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, use criteria.

230
00:15:49,829 --> 00:15:50,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, why do you eat?

231
00:15:50,889 --> 00:15:51,749
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do Buddhists say you should eat?

232
00:15:51,789 --> 00:15:53,450
[SPEAKER_01]: To be healthy, so you can practice.

233
00:15:53,490 --> 00:15:55,131
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, choose the healthy food.

234
00:15:56,168 --> 00:15:58,509
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is really horrible tasting healthy food.

235
00:15:58,949 --> 00:16:00,669
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is something I really crave about.

236
00:16:00,929 --> 00:16:03,550
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, either way, choose what's cheaper.

237
00:16:05,110 --> 00:16:06,290
[SPEAKER_01]: Or choose something in between.

238
00:16:06,771 --> 00:16:12,832
[SPEAKER_01]: Or choose what you like for making a point to not lose the mindfulness while you're eating that which you like.

239
00:16:13,332 --> 00:16:23,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if your mindfulness becomes unshakable in regard to harmless preference foods, it's nothing unwholesome about preferring a watermelon over a mango.

240
00:16:23,115 --> 00:16:24,315
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just a preference.

241
00:16:26,682 --> 00:16:27,922
[SPEAKER_01]: That does not induce lust.

242
00:16:28,142 --> 00:16:29,403
[SPEAKER_01]: It's ignorance in regard to it.

243
00:16:29,483 --> 00:16:34,204
[SPEAKER_01]: It's ignorance in regard to the preferred object that provides you with pleasure.

244
00:16:34,244 --> 00:16:35,405
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what induces lust.

245
00:16:36,005 --> 00:16:41,306
[SPEAKER_01]: But beautiful objects, tasty objects, nice sounds, none of that is actual lust.

246
00:16:41,787 --> 00:16:42,987
[SPEAKER_01]: And Buddha was quite clear on that.

247
00:16:44,150 --> 00:16:48,233
[SPEAKER_01]: So, choose whatever you're going to choose for whatever reasons, because that's beside the point.

248
00:16:48,553 --> 00:16:52,216
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, eat it mindfully when you eat it, and be mindful when you finish eating it.

249
00:16:52,656 --> 00:16:53,857
[SPEAKER_01]: And never compromise that.

250
00:16:54,757 --> 00:17:04,964
[SPEAKER_01]: So, soon enough, it won't take long, if you really commit to that sort of practice, you will basically... you'll stop caring what you eat, because you realize that was never the point.

251
00:17:06,045 --> 00:17:08,126
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, like, never, never relates to the problem.

252
00:17:08,166 --> 00:17:08,907
[SPEAKER_01]: It was mindfulness.

253
00:17:08,967 --> 00:17:13,410
[SPEAKER_01]: It was basically appropriating the domain of the taste when you're swallowing the food.

254
00:17:14,238 --> 00:17:22,604
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, entertain the notion that you are actually consuming that food, whereby when that food disappears down your throat, when you swallow it, it's in you.

255
00:17:22,964 --> 00:17:23,505
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not.

256
00:17:23,625 --> 00:17:30,450
[SPEAKER_01]: That food, when it's in your stomach, it's as external as it was when it was out on the shelf, in the shop.

257
00:17:31,871 --> 00:17:34,673
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means your stomach, then, cannot be appropriated.

258
00:17:35,113 --> 00:17:38,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Means the whole domain of your body, then, remains external to you.

259
00:17:38,960 --> 00:17:40,861
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where the mindfulness gets established upon.

260
00:17:41,442 --> 00:17:44,504
[SPEAKER_01]: And that means, you basically, you stepped outside of the whole domain of sensuality.

261
00:17:45,084 --> 00:17:49,667
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality, the domain whereby pleasure that pertains to senses can't pull you.

262
00:17:50,448 --> 00:17:52,469
[SPEAKER_01]: By being... by stepping outside, they can't pull you anymore.

263
00:17:53,270 --> 00:17:57,933
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the hollow preferences remain, but none of them induce any lust anymore.

264
00:17:57,953 --> 00:17:58,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

265
00:17:58,093 --> 00:17:58,753
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're not there.

266
00:17:59,054 --> 00:18:00,074
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't be affected by it.

267
00:18:00,314 --> 00:18:02,776
[SPEAKER_00]: There's mindfulness of that situation.

268
00:18:02,796 --> 00:18:02,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

269
00:18:02,956 --> 00:18:08,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, mind... having your mindfulness established, having your mind composed in regard to that

270
00:18:09,088 --> 00:18:21,832
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, in regards to the right extent of that phenomenon of the body, this body here and now... Because most people think like, you know, under your skin it's inside.

271
00:18:23,312 --> 00:18:27,653
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you know, your lungs are inside, your stomach is, your mouth is kind of inside.

272
00:18:28,393 --> 00:18:32,535
[SPEAKER_01]: And that inside inevitably has that, like, personal connotations.

273
00:18:32,615 --> 00:18:34,055
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in that mind.

274
00:18:35,728 --> 00:18:40,291
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, for an island, that kind of internal disappears, so to speak.

275
00:18:41,232 --> 00:18:55,401
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes it's used in a sort of different internal-external, but when the Buddha says what is left, it's this conscious body, I, out there, this conscious lump of flesh in the world, externally, and the name and matter, externally.

276
00:18:57,703 --> 00:19:04,067
[SPEAKER_01]: So whichever way he looks, internally or externally, basically all he sees is external and slightly less external, but still external, i.e.

277
00:19:04,447 --> 00:19:05,028
[SPEAKER_01]: not mine.

278
00:19:05,934 --> 00:19:07,175
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean by internal here.

279
00:19:07,195 --> 00:19:08,335
[SPEAKER_01]: It needs to be clarified.

280
00:19:08,856 --> 00:19:10,577
[SPEAKER_01]: Because internal is taken as mind.

281
00:19:10,717 --> 00:19:11,177
[SPEAKER_01]: Internal.

282
00:19:11,357 --> 00:19:11,717
[SPEAKER_01]: Fear.

283
00:19:12,198 --> 00:19:14,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering arises, like, you know, the true dukkha.

284
00:19:15,179 --> 00:19:16,820
[SPEAKER_01]: The pure dukkha arises internally.

285
00:19:17,701 --> 00:19:20,903
[SPEAKER_01]: In that, like, sense of mind, you're affected by it.

286
00:19:21,003 --> 00:19:21,203
Okay.

287
00:19:21,383 --> 00:19:21,803
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind.

288
00:19:22,163 --> 00:19:22,784
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

289
00:19:23,304 --> 00:19:24,885
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind is regarded as inside.

290
00:19:26,226 --> 00:19:31,769
[SPEAKER_01]: And you reckon, well, okay, it's inside in relation to what you see from your senses, but all of it, as a whole, it's outside.

291
00:19:32,887 --> 00:19:37,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, here, yonder, yonder and here, as in inside, are all other.

292
00:19:37,509 --> 00:20:00,738
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, there's all sorts of strange practices, like, yeah... Well, we... At least, because... we're trying to keep, you know, you're suffering in regard to food, in regard to your desires, and so you... you create a view that food is an illusion.

293
00:20:01,574 --> 00:20:10,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you blame the food or you basically, you're so caught up in the desire around the food, you end up just thinking about the food all the time.

294
00:20:10,721 --> 00:20:12,043
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, trying to get beyond.

295
00:20:13,264 --> 00:20:14,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Denying food.

296
00:20:14,284 --> 00:20:15,405
[SPEAKER_00]: Trying to replace the food.

297
00:20:15,425 --> 00:20:15,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

298
00:20:16,706 --> 00:20:18,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Something else.

299
00:20:19,569 --> 00:20:25,113
[SPEAKER_00]: And one thing that some people like to replace with is breathing.

300
00:20:26,374 --> 00:20:27,055
[SPEAKER_00]: Breatharians.

301
00:20:27,515 --> 00:20:28,236
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I heard about that.

302
00:20:28,256 --> 00:20:29,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, that's another one.

303
00:20:29,337 --> 00:20:29,757
[SPEAKER_00]: Interesting.

304
00:20:30,525 --> 00:20:31,426
[SPEAKER_01]: But what's the difference then?

305
00:20:31,446 --> 00:20:32,867
[SPEAKER_01]: Because then you just take your breath?

306
00:20:33,647 --> 00:20:36,429
[SPEAKER_00]: As a person of mind, you can't depend on it.

307
00:20:37,189 --> 00:20:41,572
[SPEAKER_00]: You have... there is... the body is dependent on any nutriment.

308
00:20:41,892 --> 00:20:42,713
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

309
00:20:42,793 --> 00:20:50,198
[SPEAKER_00]: And to say that it's, again, it's an illusion... Well, no, that doesn't make sense.

310
00:20:50,238 --> 00:20:51,058
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just a denial.

311
00:20:51,599 --> 00:20:52,439
[SPEAKER_00]: Complete denial.

312
00:20:53,880 --> 00:20:56,602
[SPEAKER_00]: And then... but breathing just air to nourish the body.

313
00:20:57,208 --> 00:20:59,589
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe some people can get better again, so...

314
00:20:59,609 --> 00:21:00,609
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's what... that's what I mean.

315
00:21:00,629 --> 00:21:01,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Why we stop there, then?

316
00:21:01,850 --> 00:21:04,111
[SPEAKER_01]: If you deny food, why don't you start denying breath?

317
00:21:04,511 --> 00:21:05,491
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is what people did.

318
00:21:05,511 --> 00:21:05,651
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

319
00:21:05,852 --> 00:21:07,052
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is what the Buddha tried to do.

320
00:21:07,492 --> 00:21:08,793
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, stop... stop breathing.

321
00:21:09,833 --> 00:21:11,914
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize, no, this is a completely wrong way.

322
00:21:13,255 --> 00:21:16,376
[SPEAKER_01]: You will never reach, well, freedom and liberation by doing it.

323
00:21:17,136 --> 00:21:18,857
[SPEAKER_01]: So what do you... like, you know, imagine...

324
00:21:18,877 --> 00:21:26,040
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, imagine the hardcore ascetic on a brink of death all the time, basically.

325
00:21:28,035 --> 00:21:32,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Has already, you know, is his close friend, and we're the same, we have the same values.

326
00:21:33,197 --> 00:21:36,559
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he goes off and eats this milk rice given to him by a woman.

327
00:21:38,059 --> 00:21:45,342
[SPEAKER_01]: Just... and he eats this bowl of probably quite delicious, tasty, healthy, fatty milk rice.

328
00:21:45,803 --> 00:21:46,803
[SPEAKER_01]: And they see him doing this.

329
00:21:49,051 --> 00:21:51,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Also, he took two oranges and said, where is your strength?

330
00:21:52,193 --> 00:22:00,079
[SPEAKER_01]: He ate the milk ruts as a form of ascetic, when he realized, actually, no, this is going to be a waste of time.

331
00:22:00,619 --> 00:22:01,520
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to go the other way.

332
00:22:05,042 --> 00:22:08,244
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because there's no wisdom in it.

333
00:22:08,344 --> 00:22:15,249
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's just like a reaction in regards... and that's what it's... the simile of the vase.

334
00:22:15,449 --> 00:22:16,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Imagine one entire vase.

335
00:22:19,616 --> 00:22:20,277
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it says.

336
00:22:21,758 --> 00:22:27,621
[SPEAKER_01]: The first herd, basically, trapped and controlled by Mahārāja, and he does what he wants.

337
00:22:27,721 --> 00:22:37,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, some other herd will actually see that, and they will recognize, oh, look, Mahārāja is actually there, and he traps people, and that is bad.

338
00:22:38,228 --> 00:22:42,371
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what they do, they shun debate completely.

339
00:22:42,700 --> 00:22:47,582
[SPEAKER_01]: In night, they run away from into the mountain when there is no pain, there is no mud, there is nothing.

340
00:22:47,602 --> 00:22:51,204
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, yeah, if you don't eat food, you will not be bothered by preferences in regard to food.

341
00:22:51,784 --> 00:22:52,945
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just throw the whole thing.

342
00:22:54,085 --> 00:22:55,165
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't sustain that.

343
00:22:56,106 --> 00:23:07,791
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha himself said that so many times, which means then, that hurt eventually, when there isn't anything left in those mountains and they, you know, start starving, they go back down.

344
00:23:08,755 --> 00:23:25,640
[SPEAKER_01]: Then the third herd sees the first group, sees the second group, and then realizes, well, what if we stay within the bait, and eat it wherever?

345
00:23:27,881 --> 00:23:30,762
[SPEAKER_01]: Then we step ahead and say, well, then they start arguing over views.

346
00:23:32,318 --> 00:23:36,441
[SPEAKER_01]: They might be, eh, living with wrong from sensuality, they start arguing over views and all kind of that.

347
00:23:36,741 --> 00:23:40,404
[SPEAKER_01]: And Mahārāja finds that, so then the false head doesn't even argue over the views.

348
00:23:41,004 --> 00:23:45,447
[SPEAKER_01]: But then the principle in terms of sensuality is basically staying within the bait, because you have no choice, really.

349
00:23:45,927 --> 00:23:47,969
[SPEAKER_01]: And by shunning the bait, you give power to the bait.

350
00:23:48,969 --> 00:23:52,932
[SPEAKER_01]: So, staying within the bait, but never ever eating it unwary.

351
00:23:53,832 --> 00:23:59,316
[SPEAKER_01]: So, literally, imagine that for the rest of your life, whatever food you put in your mouth,

352
00:24:00,737 --> 00:24:02,518
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how would you eat that food?

353
00:24:02,538 --> 00:24:04,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Very carefully, for the rest of your life.

354
00:24:04,438 --> 00:24:06,919
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to put it in your mouth, piece by piece, bit by bit.

355
00:24:06,939 --> 00:24:07,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't let it go around.

356
00:24:07,700 --> 00:24:09,600
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't lose mindfulness and just bite in it.

357
00:24:30,704 --> 00:24:31,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it will rip your mouth.

358
00:24:33,046 --> 00:24:38,129
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, oh, there is a bait, but you haven't been eating like a maniac, carelessly.

359
00:24:38,749 --> 00:24:43,012
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can feel it, you can pull it out, without actually harming yourself, you can eat the rest.

360
00:24:43,452 --> 00:24:46,795
[SPEAKER_01]: And, for the rest of your life, you're going to eat everything you put in your mouth.

361
00:24:48,796 --> 00:24:50,217
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's, that's all that happens.

362
00:24:51,537 --> 00:24:55,299
[SPEAKER_01]: And that scene of crossing the desert, that's exactly what it says.

363
00:24:55,959 --> 00:24:57,200
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you really have to eat.

364
00:24:57,941 --> 00:25:08,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, well, we have to kill this child, because he won't survive either way, but at least he will give us a chance to survive, and then maybe have some more children, and then we'll implement and we'll come up with these children, or whatever.

365
00:25:08,827 --> 00:25:12,509
[SPEAKER_01]: It's obviously the best of the horrible scenario, but...

366
00:25:13,649 --> 00:25:16,111
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you might quite like that child.

